```c
sdb_ns_set (binfile->sdb, "info", o->kv);
```

And in `bin_elf.inc` in `load_bytes`:
```c
sdb_ns_set (sdb, "info", res->kv);
```

Both call `sdb_ns_set` with "info" and `res->kv` (or `o->kv`). Looking at how `sdb_ns_set` works in ns.c:

```c
SDB_API int sdb_ns_set (Sdb *s, const char *name, Sdb *r) {
    ...
    ls_foreach (s->ns, it, ns) {
        if (ns->hash == hash) {
            if (ns->sdb == r) {
                return 0;  // Same sdb, no change
            }
            sdb_free (ns->sdb);  // Free old one
            r->refs++;
            ns->sdb = r;
            return 1;
        }
    }
    ...
    ns = R_NEW (SdbNs);
    ns->name = strdup (name);
    ns->hash = hash;
    ns->sdb = r;
    r->refs++;
    ls_append (s->ns, ns);
    return 1;
}
```

So when `load_bytes` is called, `sdb` parameter is `binfile->sdb`. The ELF plugin calls `sdb_ns_set(sdb, "info", res->kv)` which sets `binfile->sdb->ns["info"] = res->kv` and increments `res->kv->refs`.

Then in `obj.c`, after `load_bytes` succeeds, if the plugin uses `load` instead of `load_bytes`, it does `sdb_ns_set(binfile->sdb, "info", o->kv)`.

